A Promise represents a value that will be available in the future. It can be pending, fulfilled, or rejected.

```javascript
// Create a promise
const fetchUser = (id) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
        if (id > 0) resolve({ id, name: 'Alice' });
        else reject(new Error('Invalid ID'));
    }, 1000);
});

// Chain promises
fetchUser(1)
    .then(user => {
        console.log(user.name);  // 'Alice'
        return fetchPosts(user.id);  // Return another promise
    })
    .then(posts => {
        console.log(posts.length);
    })
    .catch(err => {
        console.error('Error:', err.message);
    })
    .finally(() => {
        console.log('Done');  // Runs regardless
    });

// Modern: async/await (cleaner syntax, same thing)
async function loadUser(id) {
    try {
        const user = await fetchUser(id);
        const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id);
        return { user, posts };
    } catch (err) {
        console.error('Error:', err.message);
    }
}
```

Key: `.then()` returns a new Promise, enabling chaining. `async/await` is syntactic sugar over Promises — use it for cleaner code.

Destructuring extracts values from arrays or properties from objects into variables.

```javascript
// Object destructuring
const user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30, role: 'admin' };
const { name, age } = user;
console.log(name, age);  // 'Alice' 30

// Rename
const { name: userName, role: userRole } = user;

// Default values
const { name, email = 'none' } = user;  // email defaults to 'none'

// Nested
const { address: { city } } = { address: { city: 'Brașov' } };

// Array destructuring
const [first, second, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
// first=1, second=2, rest=[3,4,5]

// Skip elements
const [, , third] = [1, 2, 3];  // third=3

// Swap variables
let a = 1, b = 2;
[a, b] = [b, a];  // a=2, b=1

// Function parameters
function createUser({ name, email, role = 'user' }) {
    return { name, email, role };
}
createUser({ name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' });
```

Destructuring makes code shorter and more readable. Use it in function parameters, import statements, and when working with API responses.

Both use `...` syntax but serve opposite purposes.

**Spread (expand):**
```javascript
// Array spread
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const arr2 = [...arr1, 4, 5];     // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
const copy = [...arr1];            // Shallow copy

// Object spread
const user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };
const updated = { ...user, age: 31 };  // { name: 'Alice', age: 31 }
const merged = { ...defaults, ...overrides };  // Merge objects

// Function call
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
Math.max(...nums);  // Same as Math.max(1, 2, 3)
```

**Rest (collect):**
```javascript
// Function parameters
function sum(...numbers) {
    return numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4);  // 10

// Destructuring rest
const { name, ...rest } = { name: 'Alice', age: 30, role: 'admin' };
// name='Alice', rest={ age: 30, role: 'admin' }

const [first, ...others] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
// first=1, others=[2, 3, 4]
```

Spread = unpack. Rest = pack. Spread appears on the right side of `=`, rest on the left side or in function parameters.

Every JavaScript object has a hidden `[[Prototype]]` link to another object. When you access a property that doesn't exist on the object, JS looks up the prototype chain.

```javascript
// Modern: class syntax (sugar over prototypes)
class Animal {
    constructor(name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    speak() {
        return `${this.name} makes a sound`;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    speak() {
        return `${this.name} barks`;
    }
    fetch(item) {
        return `${this.name} fetches ${item}`;
    }
}

const dog = new Dog('Rex');
dog.speak();        // 'Rex barks' (own method)
dog.fetch('ball');  // 'Rex fetches ball'

// Prototype chain:
// dog → Dog.prototype → Animal.prototype → Object.prototype → null

console.log(dog instanceof Dog);    // true
console.log(dog instanceof Animal); // true
```

**Under the hood:** `class` is syntactic sugar. `Dog.prototype.__proto__ === Animal.prototype`. Method lookup walks the chain until found or reaches `null`.

**Key difference from classical inheritance:** objects inherit from objects (not classes from classes). JavaScript doesn't copy methods — it links to them via the prototype chain.

**Map:** key-value pairs where keys can be ANY type (not just strings).
```javascript
const map = new Map();
map.set('name', 'Alice');
map.set(42, 'number key');
map.set(userObj, 'object as key!');

map.get('name');      // 'Alice'
map.has(42);          // true
map.size;             // 3
map.delete(42);

for (const [key, value] of map) {
    console.log(key, value);
}
```

**Set:** collection of unique values.
```javascript
const set = new Set([1, 2, 2, 3, 3]);
console.log(set);    // Set {1, 2, 3}
set.add(4);
set.has(2);          // true
set.size;            // 4

// Remove duplicates from array
const unique = [...new Set([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])];
// [1, 2, 3]
```

**WeakMap / WeakSet:** keys must be objects, and references are weak — if no other reference to the key exists, it's garbage collected.
```javascript
const cache = new WeakMap();
let user = { name: 'Alice' };
cache.set(user, expensiveComputation(user));
user = null;  // user object can now be GC'd, WeakMap entry auto-removed
```

Use WeakMap for: caches tied to object lifetime, private data on DOM elements, metadata that should disappear when the object does.

**Debounce:** wait until user STOPS doing something for N ms, then execute once.
```javascript
function debounce(fn, delay) {
    let timer;
    return (...args) => {
        clearTimeout(timer);
        timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
    };
}

// Search: only fires 300ms after user stops typing
const search = debounce((query) => {
    fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`).then(/* ... */);
}, 300);

input.addEventListener('input', (e) => search(e.target.value));
```

**Throttle:** execute at most once every N ms, even if called more often.
```javascript
function throttle(fn, limit) {
    let waiting = false;
    return (...args) => {
        if (waiting) return;
        fn(...args);
        waiting = true;
        setTimeout(() => { waiting = false; }, limit);
    };
}

// Scroll: fires at most every 100ms
window.addEventListener('scroll', throttle(() => {
    updateScrollPosition();
}, 100));
```

**When to use:**
- **Debounce:** search input, window resize, form validation
- **Throttle:** scroll events, mouse move, API polling

Debounce waits for calm. Throttle limits frequency.

**Optional chaining `?.`** — safely access nested properties without checking each level for null/undefined.

```javascript
const user = { name: 'Alice', address: { city: 'Brașov' } };

// Without ?. (verbose)
const city = user && user.address && user.address.city;

// With ?. (clean)
const city = user?.address?.city;  // 'Brașov'
const zip = user?.address?.zip;    // undefined (no error!)

// Works with methods and arrays
user?.getName?.();       // Call if method exists
users?.[0]?.name;        // Access array element safely
```

**Nullish coalescing `??`** — default value only for `null` or `undefined` (not for `0`, `''`, `false`).

```javascript
// || treats 0, '', false as falsy (often wrong)
const count = 0 || 10;      // 10 (wrong! 0 is valid)
const name = '' || 'default'; // 'default' (wrong! '' may be intentional)

// ?? only defaults on null/undefined
const count = 0 ?? 10;      // 0 (correct!)
const name = '' ?? 'default'; // '' (correct!)
const val = null ?? 'default'; // 'default'
const val = undefined ?? 42;  // 42
```

**Combined:** `const city = user?.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';`
Safely access nested property, default to 'Unknown' if null/undefined.

```javascript
const users = [
    { name: 'Alice', age: 30, active: true },
    { name: 'Bob', age: 17, active: true },
    { name: 'Carol', age: 25, active: false },
    { name: 'Dave', age: 35, active: true },
];

// map: transform each element → new array
const names = users.map(u => u.name);
// ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Carol', 'Dave']

// filter: keep elements matching condition
const adults = users.filter(u => u.age >= 18);
// [Alice, Carol, Dave]

// find: first element matching condition
const bob = users.find(u => u.name === 'Bob');
// { name: 'Bob', age: 17, active: true }

// some: does ANY element match?
const hasMinors = users.some(u => u.age < 18);  // true

// every: do ALL elements match?
const allActive = users.every(u => u.active);    // false

// reduce: combine into single value
const totalAge = users.reduce((sum, u) => sum + u.age, 0);
// 107

// Chaining
const activeAdultNames = users
    .filter(u => u.active && u.age >= 18)
    .map(u => u.name)
    .sort();
// ['Alice', 'Dave']
```

These are functional programming basics. They don't mutate the original array — they return new arrays. Use them instead of manual `for` loops for cleaner, more readable code.

**Template literals** use backticks for string interpolation and multiline strings.

```javascript
const name = 'Alice';
const age = 30;

// Interpolation
const greeting = `Hello, ${name}! You are ${age} years old.`;

// Expressions inside ${}
const msg = `Total: $${(price * quantity).toFixed(2)}`;

// Multiline
const html = `
    <div class="card">
        <h2>${name}</h2>
        <p>Age: ${age}</p>
    </div>
`;
```

**Tagged templates** — a function that processes the template:
```javascript
function sql(strings, ...values) {
    // strings: ['SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ', ' AND role = ', '']
    // values: [userId, role]
    const query = strings.join('?');
    return { query, params: values };
}

const userId = 123;
const role = 'admin';
const result = sql`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${userId} AND role = ${role}`;
// { query: 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? AND role = ?', params: [123, 'admin'] }
```

Tagged templates are used for: SQL query builders (preventing injection), CSS-in-JS (styled-components), i18n, syntax highlighting. The `html` tag in lit-html uses this pattern.
